-10-20T08:16:23.195471+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T08:16:23.195471+00:00", "detail": {}, "foreman_note": "Recurrence filed by the lore-dogfood lane, run 14 (2026-10-20). Predecessor LORE-063 (status complete, fix 615fda1, created_by lore-dogfood-run12) is byte-untouched by this run; its doc half was re-fixed in-run (literal 510 -> 523 in the same file). THE PREMISE IS NOT THE NUMBER \u2014 it is the guard exemption that hides it, verified directly: `python3 scripts/count_sweep.py 523 .` = rc 0 with the stale literal on disk.", "completed_at": "2026-10-21T17:33:00Z", "commit_hash": "797b590", "guard_result": "PASS", "evidence": "Commit 797b590 (count_sweep.py + 6 doc files + 5 new tests). Gitreins verdict 41fb5874 PASS/COMPLETE. 536 tests pass, ruff clean, count guard PASS."}
+-{"id": "LORE-067", "title": "[P3] `lore audit`'s coverage matrix has no incident data path: `runbook_compiled` is the literal `True` (lore/__main__.py:592) and the class list is the literal `SEED_CLASSES` tuple (lore/classes.py:38), so PRD US-3's \"every failure class seen in the last 90 days\" / \"the classes with incidents but no runbook\" and SC-2's \"runs on the fleet's last 90 days of incidents\" are unmet — coverage is a registry size, not a measurement", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14b", "capability_tags": ["python", "verification"], "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:23:15.059112+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T07:15:00Z", "detail": {}, "foreman_note": "filed by the dogfood lane (tick lore-dogfood-2026-10-20-08-02-03, second angle 14b) after the same-tick run-14 lane (rows PERF-2/LORE-066/LORE-063-R2, HEAD 6de0660) ceded nothing here: run 14 covered the ledger's cost and the documented install path, this row is the coverage-data-path angle. NOT a duplicate of LORE-064 (P1, freshness signal existed but was unwired; fixed by c63b82d) or LORE-065 (US-4 sync) — grep of the board found no row naming SEED_CLASSES/_CHECKS/a missing incident input. Id minted per family shape: board_next_id.py <board> LORE returns the date-shaped successor LORE-20261009 (this board carries two LORE id shapes), so the small-class successor was grep-confirmed absent in BOTH id shapes (spaced and compact) before the append, and the append script re-asserted it at write time. Evidence: docs/qa/evidence/run14b/.", "reasoning": "GAP: `lore audit`'s \"coverage + freshness matrix\" (PRD US-3) has NO incident input of any kind, and one of its four columns is a constant. The matrix is therefore a report about the shipped registry, not a measurement of the fleet's incidents — so PRD US-3's \"every failure class seen in the last 90 days\" and \"the classes with incidents but no runbook\", and SC-2's \"`lore audit` runs on the fleet's last 90 days of incidents\", are unmet at HEAD 6de0660. This is a PRODUCT-capability gap (a data pipeline that does not exist), not a doc-wording defect, so Bane's doc severity ruling (doc rows at P4/P5) does not apply; the closest filed analogue is LORE-065 (P3, US-4 covered only by code-distribution), not LORE-064 (P1, a mechanism that existed but was left unwired — that one needed reconnecting, this one needs building).\n\nTHE PROMISE (docs/PRD.md):\n- PRD.md:10 — \"Compiles the fleet's *actual* incident history — DuckBrain namespaces, board events, post-mortem docs — into living runbooks per failure class\".\n- PRD.md:28 (US-3) — \"I run `lore audit` and see: every failure class seen in the last 90 days, whether a runbook exists, whether its commands *still work* (last-validated date), and the classes with incidents but no runbook — coverage as a number, not a feeling.\"\n- PRD.md:63 (SC-2) — \"`lore audit` runs on the fleet's last 90 days of incidents and produces an honest coverage matrix (≥8 failure classes, gaps named, not hidden).\"\n\nWHAT SHIPS (executed at HEAD 6de0660, 2026-10-20):\n  .venv/bin/python -m lore audit --format json\n  -> {\"classes\":[{class_id,name,runbook_compiled,check_count,command_count,last_validated,status,...}, x14],\n      \"summary\":{\"total_classes\":14,\"classes_with_zero_real_commands\":1,\"stale_classes\":0,\"note\":...}}\n  .venv/bin/python -m lore audit --format md   -> the same 14 rows + summary prose\n\nLOCI (the whole data path, both ends literal):\n  lore/__main__.py:583   `for rb in compile_all():`                      <- audit rows come from compile_all() and nothing else\n  lore/__main__.py:592   `\"runbook_compiled\": True,`                     <- HARD-CODED LITERAL: never False, for any class, ever\n  lore/compiler.py:985   `def compile_all(...)`                          <- iterates the registry\n  lore/compiler.py:954   `for c in _CHECKS.get(cls.id, [])`              <- runbook bodies are the literal table\n  lore/compiler.py:76    `_CHECKS: dict[str, list[Check]] = {`           <- 13 hand-authored check sets, 1,400+ lines of literals\n  lore/classes.py:38     `SEED_CLASSES: tuple[FailureClass, ...] = (`    <- 13 hand-authored classes\n  lore/classes.py:543    `_REGISTRY = ClassRegistry(SEED_CLASSES + (UNCLASSIFIED_CLASS,))`  <- total_classes == 14 is a tuple length\n\nDISCRIMINATING PROBE (the reason this is a finding and not an inference — incident/ledger data has ZERO effect on the matrix):\n  $ cd /tmp/lore-df14b/empty2            # empty dir: no repo, no .lore/ ledger, no incident data at all\n  $ /home/kara/get-h3/lore/.venv/bin/python -m lore audit --format json\n  -> rc=0, total_classes=14,\n     ids=gateway-drain-window,shared-checkout-collision,secret-env-clobber,disk-pressure-corruption,cooldown-pin-drift,key-rotation-expiry,guard-degradation,spawn-hot-loop,ingest-backfill-gap,gateway-guard-violation,docs-count-drift,worktree-reap-data-loss,fast-forward-push-reject,unclassified\n  $ cd /home/kara/get-h3/lore && .venv/bin/python -m lore audit --format json   # repo WITH its 3,161-row .lore/validate-history.jsonl\n  -> IDENTICAL summary and IDENTICAL class list (the only history-driven field is `status`/`stale_reason`, the LORE-064 wiring)\n  $ grep -rniE 'incident|duckbrain|logsey' lore/*.py\n  -> ONLY (a) literal evidence `detail` strings inside _CHECKS, (b) lore/__main__.py:461 `parse_logsey_export(trail_text)` used by\n     `absorb` on a CALLER-SUPPLIED trail, (c) help text. Nothing reads incidents at audit time.\n  So the ingestion machinery exists (absorb --window/--trail-file) but is wired to the WRITE path (proposals), never to the\n  READ path (coverage). A class the fleet actually hits that is not in SEED_CLASSES lands in `unclassified` and cannot move\n  any number: total_classes is 14 whether the fleet had zero incidents or ten thousand.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, stated precisely so the fixer is not misled:\n- \"classes with incidents but no runbook\" is STRUCTURALLY UNREACHABLE: `runbook_compiled` is the literal True (line 592), so the\n  gap column the story promises can never print False. There is no third state (no-runbook / never-seen / seen-but-unclassified).\n- SC-2's \"≥8 failure classes\" is satisfied trivially and permanently by a Python tuple — it cannot fail and cannot rise when the\n  fleet learns a new class; it is the registry size, not a measurement over 90 days. `lore audit` never opens an incident source,\n  so SC-2 as written is not passable at HEAD (run 13 graded SC-2 PASS; that grade read the registry listing as the coverage matrix\n  — the re-grade above is the second angle on the same criterion, not a new criterion).\n\nWHAT IS HONEST TODAY (do not overwrite this in the fix):\n- README.md:390-403 (Shipped bullet) says \"the PRD US-3 coverage + freshness matrix: per **registry class** …\" — the word\n  \"registry\" is the honest scope, and the `last_validated`/`no data` honesty prose there is correct and should stay.\n- `_cmd_audit`'s own docstring (lore/__main__.py:616) says \"One row per registry class\". The code declares its scope; US-3/SC-2 is\n  what promises more. The defect is the gap between them, and the fix is only honest if it either closes the gap (below) or\n  restates the promise.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (pick one explicitly; do not silently narrow the PRD in a doc-only edit without filing the capability gap):\n (A) Close the gap: give `audit` an incident source (the same trail shape `absorb` already parses, plus a board/DuckBrain reader),\n     compute a real per-class `incidents_seen` count over a `--window` (default 90d), let `runbook_compiled` be COMPUTED\n     (False when a class has checks but no curated runbook entry), and add the classes that appear in incidents with no registry\n     entry as explicit named gap rows. Then SC-2 becomes falsifiable.\n (B) Restate the promise: rewrite PRD.md:28/63 and the README one-liner (README.md:10, \"actual incident history\") to the shipped\n     scope AND file the ingestion capability as its own row. Restating without filing leaves the same gap, one indirection deeper.\n Either way, delete nothing from the honesty prose: `last_validated` must keep reporting `no data` until a human attests.\n\nACCEPTANCE (reviewer's falsifiable test — one of these two must hold, and today neither does):\n (A-branch) With an incident trail containing two classes the registry does not carry, `lore audit --format json` names both as\n     gap rows and `summary.total_classes` exceeds 14; with an empty incident source it reports `incidents_seen: 0` honestly rather\n     than a 14-class coverage claim; and `runbook_compiled` is false for a class the registry lists but `_CHECKS` does not carry\n     (construct one on a scratch registry, which is why this is a test-visible contract and not a demo).\n (B-branch) No sentence in PRD.md US-3/SC-2 (PRD.md:28, PRD.md:63) or README.md:10 claims incident coverage that `lore audit`\n     does not compute; the capability gap has its own row; and README's Shipped bullet still says \"per registry class\".\n\nEVIDENCE: docs/qa/evidence/run14b/audit-no-incident-input.txt (empty-dir vs repo discriminator, raw JSON both sides),\ndocs/qa/evidence/run14b/loci.txt (file:line list + the grep census). Perf note for the fixer: the matrix is cheap today\n(0.11s warm x3, docs/qa/evidence/run14b/timing.txt; PERF-2 already owns the 14x-per-class ledger re-read that this row's\noption (A) must not make worse — wire the incident source ONCE per audit run).\n\nch:trace row=LORE-067 spec=docs/PRD.md#US-3 spec2=docs/PRD.md#Success-criteria-SC-2 wave=none:dogfood-run14b test=tests/:none-covers-audit-incident-coverage doc=README.md#L390 evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14b/audit-no-incident-input.txt witness=lore-dogfood-run14b-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:row-filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14b", "acceptance_criteria": "ACCEPTANCE (branch A, state it): with an incident trail carrying two classes outside SEED_CLASSES, the audit matrix names both as gap rows: `lore audit --format json` output expect total_classes to exceed 14; with an empty incident source expect incidents_seen printed as 0 honestly; runbook_compiled expect computed false for a registry class with no curated checks (scratch-registry test pins it). Branch B instead rewrites PRD.md US-3/SC-2 + README.md:10 to the shipped scope AND files the ingestion capability as its own row. Today NEITHER branch's conditions hold (dogfood run14b evidence) - today that is the premise, not a stale AC.", "completed_at": "2026-10-22T15:20:00+00:00", "commit_hash": "96c7cc2", "worker_summary": "Foreman-direct: wire incident data path into audit coverage matrix. Created lore/incidents.py with incident_classes()/incident_count(), modified _audit_rows() to compute runbook_compiled from incident data instead of literal True. Added incident_seen and coverage_note fields. 557 tests pass, ruff clean+format, tier-1 PASS. Tier-2 judge async job-c31210eb."}
