+ {"id": "LORE-068", "title": "[P2] this repo's own board cannot pass its own gate: `boardctl validate` returns 3 errors \u2014 LORE-050 and LORE-054 carry the out-of-vocabulary status `closed`, and events.jsonl line 177 holds id 177 before id 176 (intra-tick append-order inversion from tick lore-qa-2026-10-07-09-11-11)", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14b", "capability_tags": ["verification"], "complexity": 1, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:23:15.059112+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T14:27:34.123668+00:00", "detail": {"evidence": [{"run_id": "a862a279", "ts": "2026-10-20T14:27:34.123668+00:00"}]}, "foreman_note": "filed by the dogfood lane (tick lore-dogfood-2026-10-20-08-02-03, second angle 14b). Pre-existing: all three errors were measured BEFORE this run appended LORE-067/LORE-068, and the recorded baseline (3 errors, 42 warnings) is in the row body and docs/qa/evidence/run14b/board-validate.txt. This lane changed no board byte other than appending its own two rows. Same-tick sibling run 14 (HEAD a6984b4..6de0660) did not touch these lines. Id minted per family shape (see LORE-067's note): grep-confirmed absent in both id shapes before the append, re-asserted at write time.", "reasoning": "GAP: the repo's own board fails its own gate. `boardctl validate` cannot return OK on this checkout \u2014 3 errors, all pre-existing (none written by this run). Because the board is red before any new row is appended, every later lane that treats `RESULT: OK` as a closure criterion inherits a red baseline it cannot distinguish from damage of its own making. Filed as a BOARD-STATE defect (not a doc finding), so Bane's doc severity ruling does not apply; P2 because the gate is unusable, not P1 because nothing is lost and no user-facing path breaks.\n\nBASELINE (measured at HEAD 6de0660, BEFORE this run appended LORE-067/LORE-068; recorded as the comparison baseline for the appends):\n  $ cd /home/kara/get-h3/lore && boardctl validate\n  -> [warn] headerless board (no board.jsonl): tasks.jsonl line 1 is a task row, not board metadata \u2014 no header to validate; header counter checks skipped\n     RESULT: FAIL (3 error(s), 42 warning(s))\n\nTHE THREE ERRORS (verbatim):\n  1. tasks.jsonl line 76 (task LORE-050): status \"closed\" not in vocabulary {blocked,complete,failed,in_progress,pending,review}\n       (accepted read aliases: completed->complete, done->complete, in-progress->in_progress, in_progress->in_progress,\n        inprogress->in_progress, open->pending, reopen->pending, reopened->pending, todo->pending)\n  2. tasks.jsonl line 83 (task LORE-054): same out-of-vocabulary status \"closed\"\n  3. events.jsonl line 177: event id 176 descends below earlier id 177 (ids must ascend; gaps tolerated)\n\nEVIDENCE FOR EACH, FROM THE BYTES (not from the validator's summary):\n  $ jq -r 'select(.id==\"LORE-050\" or .id==\"LORE-054\")|[.id,.status]|@tsv' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl\n  -> LORE-050\tclosed\n     LORE-054\tclosed\n     (\"closed\" is not even a READ ALIAS \u2014 the accepted aliases are completed/done/in-progress/in_progress/inprogress/open/\n      reopen/reopened/todo \u2014 so unlike the `done` rows elsewhere on this board (LORE-051, LORE-053) it is not tolerated.)\n  $ sed -n '175,178p' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | jq -rc '{id,event_type,task_id}'\n  -> {\"id\":175,\"event_type\":\"task_created\",\"task_id\":\"SUP-DEPS-LORE-20261006\"}\n     {\"id\":177,\"event_type\":\"qa_recurrence\",\"task_id\":\"QA-LORE-2\"}\n     {\"id\":176,\"event_type\":\"qa_recurrence\",\"task_id\":\"QA-LORE-3\"}\n     {\"id\":178,\"event_type\":\"qa_recurrence\",\"task_id\":\"QA-LORE-4\"}\n  i.e. the QA lane for tick lore-qa-2026-10-07-09-11-11 wrote event 177 to disk before event 176: the two rows carry the SAME\n  tick and the SAME ts (2026-10-07T05:05:00-05:00), so the inversion is an intra-tick append-order artifact, not a bad id.\n  PROVENANCE: these are NOT this run's writes and not the dogfood run-14 lane's \u2014 the ids date to 2026-10-04..10-07 and\n  `git log --oneline -3 -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` shows the file last touched by the lore-docs lane (17b9fa9 /\n  228cb35, 2026-10-19), which appended a record without reordering the pre-existing inversion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS (do not dismiss as cosmetic):\n- This board is JSONL-canonical with a `boardctl validate` gate, and this repo's own lanes close rows on validate results. A lane\n  that appends correctly cannot tell \"42 warnings, 3 errors\" from \"42 warnings, 3 errors + its own damage\" \u2014 the pre-existing count\n  is the only thing that distinguishes them, and nothing in the repo records it (this row is the first recorded baseline).\n- The id-ascension rule is the guard that would have caught a real lost-write/duplicated-append class (the crier tasks.jsonl\n  691->1 truncation incident was caught by row-count guards of the same family). A board that ships with one live inversion\n  teaches every reader that the check is noise.\n\nFIX (one sitting, board state only \u2014 no code change):\n  1. Rewrite the two rows' status LINE-WISE, asserting each row's own id before touching it (never re-serialize tasks.jsonl):\n     LORE-050 and LORE-054 status \"closed\" -> \"complete\" (both already read as closed via prose; `complete` is the vocabulary\n     value the other closed rows on this board use).\n  2. Repair the events.jsonl ordering so ids ascend: swap the two adjacent lines 177/178 (id 176 then id 177) \u2014 or, if any reader\n     depends on file order matching id order, renumber \u2014 and re-run the validator. Do not delete either event; both are real QA\n     recurrence records for QA-LORE-2 / QA-LORE-3.\n  3. Record the new baseline (expected: errors 0) in the commit message so the next lane has a number to compare against.\n\nACCEPTANCE (reviewer's falsifiable test): `boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` (0 errors) on a clean checkout; the warning count\nis not HIGHER than the pre-repair 42; `jq -r 'select(.id==\"LORE-050\" or .id==\"LORE-054\")|.status' tasks.jsonl` returns `complete`\ntwice; and `awk -F'\"id\":' 'NF>1{gsub(/[^0-9].*/,\"\",$2); print $2}' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` is non-decreasing.\n\nSCOPE NOTE (this run did not fix it \u2014 read-only lane): the row is filed, the bytes are untouched, and the diagnostic trail is\nrecorded under docs/qa/evidence/run14b/board-validate.txt so the fix has a pre-image.\n\nch:trace row=LORE-068 spec=none:repo-gate-integrity wave=none:dogfood-run14b test=none:validate-is-the-gate doc=.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14b/board-validate.txt witness=lore-dogfood-run14b-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:row-filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14b\n", "commit_hash": "3d38f02", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "SKIP", "worker_summary": "Fixed 3 boardgate errors: LORE-050 status 'closed'\u2192'complete', LORE-054 status 'closed'\u2192'complete', events.jsonl id 176/177 swap to ascending order. boardctl validate \u2192 OK (0 errors, 96 warnings). Tier-2 verdict a862a279 PASS COMPLETE."}
+ {"id": "LORE-069", "title": "[P3] `lore absorb` can never emit a runbook UPDATE: its only production call site passes `existing=None` (lore/absorb.py:175), so every proposal is action \"new\" with `added` byte-identical to the runbook the class already has \u2014 the diff engine (compiler.py:993-1030) is exercised only by tests, and PRD US-2's \"drafts the runbook update ... evidence blocks, dead ends marked dead\" is unmet", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14c", "capability_tags": ["python", "cli"], "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:34:01.103700+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T14:30:00Z", "detail": {}, "foreman_note": "Filed by the lore-dogfood lane (tick lore-dogfood-2026-10-20-08-02-03, third angle run 14c). Angle: the PRD User Stories as stories \u2014 US-2 (the post-incident scribe), which run 14 (ledger cost + install) and run 14b (audit coverage) did not drive. Not a duplicate: grep of the board found no row naming propose()'s `existing`, the update action, or US-2; LORE-020 (--source dropped) and LORE-053 (fail-closed error text) both assume the proposal payload is otherwise correct, and LORE-005 (evidence-block extraction) is complete. Id minted per family shape: board_next_id.py <board> LORE returns the date-shaped successor LORE-20261009 (this board carries two LORE id shapes), so the small-class successor LORE-069 was grep-confirmed absent in BOTH id shapes (spaced and compact) and re-asserted at append time by the writing script. Read-only lane: no code changed; evidence under docs/qa/evidence/run14c/.", "reasoning": "GAP: `lore absorb` \u2014 the PRD's post-incident scribe (US-2) \u2014 can never emit a runbook UPDATE, because its only production call site asks for a proposal from scratch. Every proposal it prints carries `\"action\": \"new\"`, `\"removed\": {}`, `\"changed\": {}`, and an `added` body that is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the runbook the class already has. The diff engine that implements \"update\" is implemented and unit-tested, but no user-facing path reaches it. This is a WIRING defect of the same shape as LORE-064 (mechanism real, unreachable from the surface) \u2014 but on the scribe surface, not the audit one; it is not a duplicate of LORE-020 (that row was `--source` being dropped, fixed) or LORE-053 (the fail-closed error text), both of which assume the proposal itself is right.\n\nPRD US-2 (docs/PRD.md:26) \u2014 \"As the agent that just closed an incident, I run `lore absorb --window 2h --ns coding-hermes` and it drafts the **runbook update** from what actually happened \u2014 evidence blocks, working commands, **dead ends marked dead** \u2014 filed as a proposal the operator approves.\"\n\nEXECUTED at HEAD 29bdbef (2026-10-20), scratch CWD, real 6-block trail in the documented logsey-export shape (docs/qa/evidence/run14c/trail-real.txt; a gateway drain-503 block, a drain-window-still-open block, a guard-failure block, a docs-count-drift block, a board note, a disk-pressure block):\n\n  SWEEP path \u2014 `.venv/bin/python -m lore absorb --window 2h --trail-file trail-real.txt --ns lore --board .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl --source dogfood-dagger` (rc=0, 3 proposals)\n    {\"class_id\":\"gateway-drain-window\",\"action\":\"new\",\"removed\":{},\"changed\":{},\"added_checks\":7}\n    {\"class_id\":\"unclassified\",\"action\":\"new\",\"removed\":{},\"changed\":{},\"added_checks\":0}\n    {\"class_id\":\"gateway-guard-violation\",\"action\":\"new\",\"removed\":{},\"changed\":{},\"added_checks\":2}\n  SINGLE path \u2014 `absorb --class gateway-drain-window --lesson \"...\" --source dogfood-dagger` (rc=0)\n    {\"class_id\":\"gateway-drain-window\",\"action\":\"new\",\"removed\":{},\"changed\":{},\"keys\":\"action,added,changed,class_id,lesson,note,removed,source\"}\n  Is the proposal body just the existing runbook? `jq -s '.[0].added == .[1]'` over that payload and `lore show gateway-drain-window --format json`\n    -> **true** (byte-identical). gateway-drain-window is a curated class whose runbook has 7 checks, so \"new\" is factually wrong and the payload carries no delta at all.\n\nTHE LOCUS (two halves \u2014 a fixer who only does the first ships a label change, see below):\n  (1) NO CALLER PASSES `existing`. `grep -rn 'propose(' lore/*.py` yields exactly one production call:\n        lore/absorb.py:175    `payload = propose(class_id)`      <-- no `existing` argument\n      and `grep -rn 'existing=' lore/*.py` is EMPTY: nothing in the shipped package ever supplies the existing\n      runbook. Both surfaces funnel through this: `absorb_sweep` (lore/absorb.py:214) groups blocks and builds each\n      proposal via `absorb_proposal` (lore/absorb.py:162 -> :175), and the single path is the same function.\n      `lore/compiler.py:993` reads `def propose(class_id: str, existing: Runbook | None = None)` and sets\n      `\"action\": \"new\" if existing is None else \"update\"` (compiler.py:1003) \u2014 so `existing=None` is the whole reason\n      every payload says new, and `removed`/`changed` stay `{}` because the diff branch at compiler.py:1010 returns\n      early (`proposal[\"added\"] = fresh.to_dict(); return proposal`).\n      The engine itself works and is covered \u2014 tests DO pass `existing` (tests/test_compiler.py:182 `propose(\"gateway-drain-window\", rb)`, :200 `(\u2026, mutated)`, :213 `(\u2026, fewer)`), so this is not \"unimplemented\", it is \"unwired\".\n  (2) THE TRAIL NEVER REACHES THE COMPARED RUNBOOK. `propose()`'s fresh side is `fresh = compile_class(class_id)`\n      (lore/compiler.py:1000) \u2014 compiled from the curated registry, which never sees the swept blocks or the `lesson`.\n      So merely passing `existing=compile_class(class_id)` would produce an **update with an empty diff every time**:\n      registry-vs-registry is identical by construction. A correct fix must fold the trail's evidence (and, per US-2,\n      dead-end markers) into the FRESH side before diffing, or the \"update\" is guaranteed empty. State which half you\n      are doing in the commit; a label-only change is worse than the current honest \"new\" because it would claim an\n      update that contains nothing.\n\nUS-2's other clauses, checked at the same HEAD (same evidence file):\n  - \"evidence blocks\": the swept blocks ARE parsed and kept \u2014 but only in the proposal's provenance sub-object\n    (`sweep.evidence`, one entry per block, all 4 unclassified blocks preserved), never in the runbook body being\n    proposed (`added.evidence_trail` is still the curated trail). A scribe approving the proposal cannot see the\n    incident evidence inside it.\n  - \"dead ends marked dead\": `grep -rniE 'dead end|dead-end' lore/*.py` -> ZERO hits; the phrase occurs only in\n    docs/PRD.md:26. No dead-end marking exists in the product.\n  - \"working commands\": the proposed body carries the curated commands, which is honest \u2014 but they are the runbook's,\n    not \"what actually happened\" in the swept window.\n\nWHAT THE CLI ITSELF SAYS (why this is a promise gap and not a misreading): `lore absorb --help` describes the\nsubcommand as \"build the runbook-update PROPOSAL for an absorb decision\" (docs/INSTALL.md:135-137 pastes the same\nline) and the `--class` help reads \"existing registry class to **propose an update for**\". Both sentences are\nfalsified by every payload the command can produce.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: (i) resolve the class's existing runbook in `absorb_proposal` and pass it as `existing` so\n`action`/`changed`/`removed` become meaningful; (ii) incorporate the swept evidence into the fresh side so the diff\nhas content (this is the part that makes the feature real); (iii) render the dead-end/working-command distinction\nUS-2 names into the proposal payload (a per-block outcome field, not prose); (iv) keep propose-not-write intact \u2014\n`absorb` still writes nothing.\n\nACCEPTANCE (reviewer's falsifiable test): for a curated class with an existing runbook and a trail that adds at least\none piece of evidence, `lore absorb --window 2h --trail-file <trail>` emits `\"action\": \"update\"` with a NON-EMPTY\n`changed`/`removed`/`added` naming the trail-derived content; for a trail that adds nothing, the payload says so\nhonestly (an explicit empty update, never \"new\"); `lore absorb --class <id> --lesson <text>` on a curated class emits\n`\"action\": \"update\"`; tests pin both the update and the no-change case at the `absorb_proposal` seam (not only at\n`propose()`); and `grep -rn 'existing=' lore/*.py` no longer returns empty.\n\nEVIDENCE: docs/qa/evidence/run14c/absorb-update-never.txt (call-site census, both observed payloads, the\n`added == show` equality, the help text), docs/qa/evidence/run14c/trail-real.txt (the input),\ndocs/qa/evidence/run14c/out-real.json (the raw 3-proposal output). Perf: absorb sits at the interpreter floor\n(49.7 ms +- 1.9 warm, 0.08 s cold, docs/qa/evidence/run14c/perf.txt) \u2014 no PERF row filed for this surface.\n\nch:trace row=LORE-069 spec=docs/PRD.md#US-2 test=tests/:absorb-update-case(planned) wave=none:dogfood-run14c doc=docs/INSTALL.md#L135 evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14c/absorb-update-never.txt witness=lore-dogfood-run14c-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:row-filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14c\n", "acceptance_criteria": "ACCEPTANCE: for a curated class with an existing runbook and a trail that adds evidence, run the absorb sweep in its documented window mode (option named in `lore absorb --help`): expect action printed as update with a NON-EMPTY changed or added key naming the trail-derived content; a no-change trail expect an explicitly empty update (never new); `grep -rn 'existing=' lore` expect at least 1 production hit; tests pin both cases at the absorb_proposal seam.", "commit_hash": "3a011c2", "worker_summary": "absorb_proposal now diffs the folded trail against the existing compiled runbook, emitting action=update with a non-empty added.evidence_trail for evidence-adding trails and an explicitly empty update for no-change trails. Tests pin both cases (38 passed, 557 total).", "acceptance_verified": true}
+ {"id": "LORE-070", "title": "[P4] PRD US-1's own example does not reproduce: `lore match \"spawn 503\"` returns unclassified 0.00 (the story's quoted symptom too), while the same text resolves gateway-drain-window through `lore consult --failure` \u2014 the responder's headline story is illustrated with a command that finds nothing", "status": "pending", "priority": "P4", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14d", "capability_tags": ["docs", "classifier"], "complexity": 1, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:40:22.936430+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T07:15:00Z", "detail": {}, "foreman_note": "Filed by the lore-dogfood lane (tick lore-dogfood-2026-10-20-08-02-03, fourth angle run 14d). Angle: PRD US-1 (the 3 a.m. responder) driven as a story \u2014 runs 14 (ledger cost + install), 14b (audit coverage) and 14c (US-2 scribe/absorb) did not drive it. SCOPE GUARD for the foreman: this is a DOC example defect, NOT part of the classifier-recall family \u2014 LORE-016 / LORE-023 / REVIEW-LORE-002 / LORE-043 are all complete and this row does not ask to reopen them; its fix is the PRD example (or a one-clause pointer to the working `--failure` form). Id minted per family shape: board_next_id.py <board> LORE returns the date-shaped successor LORE-20261009 (two LORE id shapes on this board), so the small-class successor LORE-070 was grep-confirmed absent in BOTH id shapes and re-asserted at append time. Read-only lane: no code changed; evidence under docs/qa/evidence/run14d/.", "reasoning": "GAP: PRD US-1 \u2014 the product's headline story, the 3 a.m. incident responder \u2014 is illustrated with a command\nthat does not reproduce. Its own example symptom, run verbatim, returns `unclassified`; so does the story's\nquoted phrase. The class it promises is reachable from the SAME text only through a different form the example\nnever mentions. DOC-CLASS defect (a documented-but-wrong statement): Bane's 2026-09-30 ruling files doc findings\nat P4/P5, and this row asks for an example fix plus a doc note, not a classifier rewrite.\n\nTHE PROMISE (docs/PRD.md:24): \"As an agent at 3 a.m. facing \\\"scheduler spawns 503-ing at spawn time,\\\" I ask\n`lore match \\\"spawn 503\\\"` and get the drain-window runbook \u2014 checks in order, the exact logsey query, the\nknown-good command sequence\".\n\nEXECUTED at HEAD b29cbf5 (2026-10-20), repo root, generator present:\n  $ .venv/bin/python -m lore match \"spawn 503\"\n  -> unclassified     confidence=0.00    evidence: none            (rc=0)\n  $ .venv/bin/python -m lore match \"scheduler spawns 503-ing at spawn time\"\n  -> unclassified     confidence=0.00    evidence: none            (rc=0)\n  $ .venv/bin/python -m lore match \"spawn 503\" --explain\n  -> unclassified     confidence=0.00\n     near-misses:\n       gateway-drain-window  score=0.17  (1/6 keywords: 503)\n       spawn-hot-loop        score=0.17  (1/6 keywords: spawn)\n     i.e. the drain-window class IS the top near-miss, but at 0.17 it is nowhere near the gate.\n\nMECHANISM (why the example cannot work as written): gateway-drain-window is accepted on the SIGNATURE `drain 503`\nplus its keywords; \"spawn 503\" shares exactly one token (`503`) and adds a token (`spawn`) that belongs to a\nDIFFERENT class. The two candidate classes therefore tie at 0.17/0.17, below both the long-text gate\n(`KEYWORD_THRESHOLD = 0.6`) and the short-text gate (`SHORT_KEYWORD_THRESHOLD = 0.3`, lore/classifier.py:34-59).\nWith `--explain` the 3 a.m. responder at least sees the near-miss; without it (the form the PRD shows) they see\n`unclassified`, which the README teaches them to read as \"the fleet has never seen this\".\n\nTHE MODE ASYMMETRY (the useful half \u2014 a working path exists, and the docs do not say so):\n  $ .venv/bin/python -m lore consult \"spawn 503\"\n  -> no matching runbook                                           (rc=0)\n  $ .venv/bin/python -m lore consult --failure \"spawn 503 while restarting\"\n  -> this class has a runbook: gateway-drain-window (Gateway drain window) status=proposal last_validated=never checks=7\n     ...7 checks rendered with fill-in hints; rc=0\n  $ .venv/bin/python -m lore consult \"gateway drain 503\"           # control: a symptom that does match\n  -> runbook: gateway-drain-window ... checks=7\n  So the SAME symptom text resolves through `--failure` and returns nothing through the positional form. The\n  reader following US-1 verbatim (match, then plain consult) is told \"nothing\" twice, while the runbook was one\n  flag away. Nothing in PRD US-1, README's Shipped bullet for the responder, or the consult help text tells them\n  which form to use for a symptom they are pasting mid-fire.\n\nWHAT IS **NOT** BEING FILED HERE (checked so the foreman does not re-open a closed family): this is NOT a\nclassifier-recall regression. That family is filed and CLOSED \u2014 LORE-016 (stranger phrasings -> unclassified),\nLORE-023 (real incident vocabulary misses), REVIEW-LORE-002 (natural incident phrasings), LORE-043 (consult\ntitle-mode 0.375 < 0.6), all `complete`. This row's ask is the DOC-side fix plus an explicit pointer to the\nworking form; it does not ask for new keywords and must not be used to reopen those rows.\nAlso verified and NOT filed: the README's own documented example strings all reproduce exactly (drain 503 ->\ngateway-drain-window 0.90; \"secret .env clobber\" -> secret-env-clobber 0.90; \"the coffee machine is making a\nweird noise\" -> unclassified 0.00 as documented; \"gateway drain 503\" -> 0.90; \"key rotation expired\" ->\nkey-rotation-expiry 0.90 \u2014 docs/qa/evidence/run14d/match-battery.txt), so the defect is specific to the PRD's\nexample, not to the docs as a whole. US-1's other clause \u2014 \"checks in order\" \u2014 HOLDS for all 14 registry rows\n(orders contiguous from 1, ascending in list position, and identical between `compile --format json` and\n`show --format json`; docs/qa/evidence/run14d/checks-in-order.txt).\n\nFIX DIRECTION (one sitting, docs/PRD.md; pick one and say which):\n (a) replace US-1's example with a symptom the classifier accepts today AND that still reads like the story's\n     incident (e.g. `lore match \"drain 503\"`), keeping the story's narrative sentence as prose; or\n (b) keep the example and add one clause showing the working form \u2014 \"if `match` says unclassified, ask\n     `lore consult --failure \"<the line you just saw>\"`\" \u2014 which is the honest teaching moment for a 3 a.m.\n     reader and needs no code change; or\n (c) if the fleet's real phrasing (\"spawn \u2026 503\", \"scheduler spawns 503\") is worth accepting as a\n     gateway-drain-window signal, file that as its OWN row against the closed recall family, with the\n     tie-at-0.17 evidence above \u2014 do not bundle it into the doc fix.\n(a) or (b) are the cheap, complete fixes for this row; (c) is explicitly out of scope here.\n\nACCEPTANCE (reviewer's falsifiable test): running US-1's example command VERBATIM either returns\n`gateway-drain-window`, or the PRD text around it names the form that does (the `--failure` route, demonstrated);\nand no sentence in US-1 claims a result its own example does not produce. Grep-checkable corollary: the string\n`lore match \"spawn 503\"` does not appear in docs/PRD.md unless the surrounding text also shows the working path.\n\nEVIDENCE: docs/qa/evidence/run14d/us1-example-fails.txt (all arms verbatim: match, match --explain, the story's\nsentence, plain consult, consult --failure, the control), docs/qa/evidence/run14d/match-battery.txt (every\ndocumented example string + the PRD narrative strings, class per line), docs/qa/evidence/run14d/checks-in-order.txt\n(the passing invariant), docs/qa/evidence/run14d/perf.txt (match 56.2 ms +- 4.1 warm, 0.09/0.10 s cold \u2014 no PERF\nrow: at the interpreter floor).\n\nch:trace row=LORE-070 spec=docs/PRD.md#US-1 test=tests/:none-covers-prd-example-repro doc=docs/PRD.md#L24 evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14d/us1-example-fails.txt witness=lore-dogfood-run14d-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:row-filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14d\n", "acceptance_criteria": "ACCEPTANCE: running US-1's example verbatim (`lore match \"spawn 503\"`) expect either the gateway-drain-window runbook printed, or the PRD's own text naming the working consult form (the failure-text option named in `lore consult --help`) right beside the example; no sentence in US-1 claims a result its example does not produce. Re-confirm at close-out with a fresh probe of both commands and quote it."}
